CAT = "cpaaudControls"; SEG = "CTL"
Q = [
("Under the COSO Internal Control - Integrated Framework, which component is the foundation for all the others?",
 "Control environment", ["Risk assessment", "Control activities", "Monitoring activities"],
 "The control environment sets the tone of the organization: integrity, ethical values, board oversight, structure and accountability. The other four components rest on it."),
("How many principles does the 2013 COSO internal control framework set out across its five components?",
 "17", ["5", "12", "20"],
 "The 2013 framework has five components supported by 17 principles, all of which must be present and functioning for effective internal control."),
("Which duties should be kept apart for good segregation of duties?",
 "Custody, recording, authorization", ["Ordering, receiving, storing", "Billing, shipping, collecting", "Hiring, training, reviewing"],
 "Separating authorization of transactions, recording of transactions and custody of assets keeps one person from both committing and concealing an error or fraud."),
("Which is an IT general control rather than an application control?",
 "Restricting access to program code", ["A check digit on account numbers", "A limit test on employee pay rates", "A batch total of sales invoices"],
 "IT general controls cover access, program change, program development and computer operations. Check digits, limit tests and batch totals operate inside one application."),
("An edit check that rejects an hours-worked entry above 80 in a week is a:",
 "Limit test", ["Hash total", "Validity check", "Sequence check"],
 "A limit (reasonableness) test flags values outside a set bound. A hash total adds a field with no meaning, such as employee numbers, to check completeness of a batch."),
("Adding up employee ID numbers in a payroll batch to check that no record was lost or added is a:",
 "Hash total", ["Record count", "Financial total", "Check digit"],
 "A hash total sums a field that has no meaning as a total. A change in the sum shows a record was lost, added or altered."),
("A control that requires the shipping clerk to match goods to an approved sales order before release mainly addresses:",
 "Occurrence of sales", ["Valuation of receivables", "Completeness of sales", "Classification of sales"],
 "Matching shipments to approved orders helps ensure recorded sales are real transactions, which is the occurrence assertion."),
("Prenumbered shipping documents that are accounted for in sequence mainly address the:",
 "Completeness of sales", ["Occurrence of sales", "Valuation of inventory", "Rights to receivables"],
 "Accounting for every prenumbered document shows that all shipments were billed and recorded, which supports completeness."),
("In the purchasing cycle, who should approve a vendor invoice for payment?",
 "Someone outside purchasing", ["The purchasing agent who ordered", "The clerk who received the goods", "The vendor's own sales manager"],
 "Accounts payable approves payment after matching the purchase order, receiving report and invoice. The person who ordered the goods should not approve payment for them."),
("Which payroll control best prevents payments to fictitious employees?",
 "HR adds employees, not payroll", ["Payroll prepares the checks", "Supervisors hand out checks", "Overtime is approved weekly"],
 "Keeping the authority to add employees to the master file in human resources, separate from payroll processing, prevents a payroll clerk from creating ghost employees."),
("A SOC 1 Type 2 report gives a user auditor evidence about a service organization's controls that is:",
 "Design and operating effectiveness", ["Design at a single point in time", "Security and privacy controls only", "Accuracy of user entities' statements"],
 "A Type 1 report covers the fairness of the description and the suitability of design at a date. A Type 2 report adds tests of operating effectiveness over a period."),
("In a SOC 1 report, controls the service organization assumes its customers have in place are called:",
 "Complementary user entity controls", ["Compensating controls at the user", "Entity-level controls at the user", "Controls at the subservice provider"],
 "Complementary user entity controls are those the service organization's design assumes user entities have. The user auditor considers whether the client has them."),
("A service organization's report excludes the controls of its own subservice organization. This presentation is called the:",
 "Carve-out method", ["Inclusive method", "Exclusion method", "Bridge method"],
 "Under the carve-out method, the subservice organization's controls are left out of the description and tests. Under the inclusive method they are included."),
("Which is a control activity under COSO?",
 "Reconciling bank statements", ["Setting the ethics code", "Assessing fraud risk", "Evaluating internal audit"],
 "Control activities are actions set by policies and procedures, such as reconciliations, approvals and verifications. An ethics code belongs to the control environment."),
("Ongoing evaluations by internal audit of whether controls are working belong to which COSO component?",
 "Monitoring activities", ["Control environment", "Risk assessment process", "Control activities"],
 "Monitoring activities are ongoing and separate evaluations of whether each component of internal control is present and functioning."),
("A walkthrough of a transaction is mainly used to:",
 "Confirm understanding of the flow", ["Test controls over the whole year", "Project misstatements to the population", "Confirm balances with third parties"],
 "A walkthrough follows a transaction from origination through to the records. It confirms the auditor's understanding of the process and helps evaluate design and implementation."),
("A deficiency in program change controls is most likely to cause:",
 "Unauthorized changes to processing", ["Theft of physical inventory", "Late payments to vendors", "Errors in manual journal entries only"],
 "Weak program change controls let untested or unauthorized code changes reach production, so automated controls may stop working as designed."),
("A user who can both create a vendor in the master file and approve payments to it is a problem of:",
 "Segregation of duties", ["Data backup and recovery", "Physical security of servers", "Program development controls"],
 "Combining master file maintenance with payment approval lets one person set up a fictitious vendor and pay it."),
("A small entity lacks segregation of duties because it has two accounting staff. A common compensating control is:",
 "Owner review of bank statements", ["More frequent data backups", "A longer month-end close", "Using prenumbered forms alone"],
 "Active owner-manager oversight, such as reviewing bank statements and canceled checks, can compensate for limited segregation of duties."),
("Which deficiency in internal control is the most severe?",
 "Material weakness", ["Significant deficiency", "Control deficiency", "Other-matter deficiency"],
 "A material weakness gives a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement will not be prevented or detected and corrected on a timely basis. A significant deficiency is less severe."),
]
